POLE big-block read cache (re-apply after ANY re-fetch of third-party/include/pole)
===================================================================================

third-party/* is gitignored (.gitignore:20), so third-party/include/pole/pole.cpp is
NOT tracked by git even though it IS compiled into libvpinball.so
(make/CMakeLists_common_standalone.txt). Any re-provision of third-party/ silently
wipes the change below. This file is the durable copy. Same failure mode that lost
the vendored-pinmame fixes once already - see vendored-pinmame-android-fixes.patch.

Why:
  VPX reads a .vpx (an MS-CFB compound document) through POLE. StreamIO::read()
  fetches one whole big block (4096 bytes) per call and keeps NO positional cache,
  while BiffReader consumes records two to four bytes at a time. So reading a single
  4-byte BIFF token costs a full seek + read(4096) syscall, and the NEXT token in the
  same block costs another one. POLE ships a read cache (CACHEBUFSIZE, ~line 66) but
  it is only wired to getch(), which VPX never calls.

  On internal storage this is invisible. On an SD card (high random-read latency) it
  dominates table load: a 627-directory-entry table measured 5.44 s in the GameItem
  stage alone, on a device where the same table is near-instant from internal storage.

What this does:
  Adds a one-block cache to StorageIO, consulted by loadBigBlock(). Repeat reads of
  the same block are served with a memcpy instead of a syscall. Only whole-block
  reads populate it, so a short read at EOF can never satisfy a later longer request.
  Invalidated in saveBigBlocks() (any write may land on the cached block) and in
  close(). Freed in ~StorageIO().

  Truncated files: a block whose FAT entry points past end of file makes
  loadBigBlocks() return a wrapped, huge count. The bytes <= blockSize guard keeps
  that out of the cache; without it the memcpy into cacheData segfaults where stock
  POLE merely returned garbage for that block (verified 2026-09-05 on a 200 MB
  truncation of Fathom 3.0.3: segfault before, clean read after).

  Thread safety: PoleStorage::OpenStream() originally cloned an entire
  POLE::Storage per stream, which gave every thread its own StorageIO and its own
  file handle. That cloning has since been removed (one parsed document is now
  shared by the whole storage/stream tree), so this cache IS shared across
  threads. It is protected because the shim serializes every entry into POLE on
  PoleStorage's mutex - see standalone/PoleStorage.h getMutex(). Do NOT introduce
  a POLE call path that bypasses that lock.

Apply from the vpinball repo root:
  patch -p1 < patches/vendored-pole-block-cache.patch

Verify it is present after any third-party re-fetch:
  grep -c cacheBlock third-party/include/pole/pole.cpp                 # expect 4
  grep -c "bytes <= bbat->blockSize" third-party/include/pole/pole.cpp # expect 1 (truncation guard)
  patch -R -p1 --dry-run < patches/vendored-pole-block-cache.patch     # must apply cleanly = file matches this patch

History:
  2026-08-30  one-block read cache (commit bf7b99b). Per-stream POLE::Storage clone
              made it per-thread by construction.
  2026-08-30  PoleStorage::Share replaces Clone: one parsed document per table, all
              POLE entry points serialized on PoleStorage::m_pMutex. Thread-safety
              paragraph above rewritten accordingly.
  2026-09-05  bytes <= blockSize guard before caching (truncated-file segfault).
              Round-trip verified: patch -R then patch reproduces pole.cpp byte for byte.

